You've asked for practical details regarding Apache Kafka, building on the theoretical understanding we've discussed. The sources provide several insights into how Kafka is used and managed in a real-world context:
1. Running and Setting Up Kafka
•
A Kafka deployment consists of a single large cluster.
•
Within this cluster, there are multiple Kafka servers called brokers.
•
For fault tolerance and data replication, it's usually recommended to have a minimum of three brokers. This ensures that if one broker or topic dies, your data is still persisted elsewhere.
•
Traditionally (for Kafka versions up to 2.x.x), you would need to start both the Kafka server and a Zookeeper server. Kafka cannot function without Zookeeper in these versions as Zookeeper coordinates and manages the Kafka cluster.
•
However, there are ongoing efforts (like KIP-500) to reduce and eventually eliminate Kafka's dependency on Zookeeper, aiming for a self-managed metadata store within Kafka itself. This change is anticipated to be complete by Kafka 4.x, meaning you would only need to start the Kafka server.
2. Practical Producer Usage
•
When you want to send a message to Kafka, producers create a ProducerRecord instance.
•
You must specify the topic name to which the message will be sent (e.g., an "email topic" for an order service sending emails) and the value (the actual content or message) you want to send. For example, order is successful could be the value.
•
Other parameters are optional:
◦
partition: You can optionally specify which partition the record should be sent to. If not specified, Kafka uses a default partition key to determine the partition, often choosing one that is less overloaded.
◦
key: A key associated with the message can be used to determine which partition the message is sent to. If not specified, Kafka will manage this.
◦
headers: These are optional metadata to be included with the message.
•
Producers always write data to the leader broker and its leader partition for a given topic.
•
Kafka automatically handles the offset management for producers, assigning the next available unique ID to each new message within a partition.
3. Practical Consumer Usage
•
Consumers subscribe to topics and pull messages from the leader partitions.
•
Consumers are often organized into consumer groups to allow for parallel processing and load distribution of partitions. Notably, within a single consumer group, only one consumer can read from one particular partition to maintain message order. However, different consumer groups can read from the same partition.
•
Offset Management: This is a crucial practical detail handled by Kafka.
◦
As a consumer reads a message, it acknowledges the broker that it has successfully read the message.
◦
After processing a batch of messages, the consumer commits its offset to Kafka. This committed offset tells Kafka how much data the consumer has successfully processed.
◦
The primary purpose of committing offsets is to prevent duplicate reads and ensure messages are read in order. If a consumer fails and then restarts, Kafka tells it to begin reading from the last committed offset, not from the beginning, thereby avoiding re-processing already handled messages. This is why you don't typically see duplicate logs in tools like Splunk.
4. Deployment, Scalability, and Integration Examples
•
Scalability: Kafka is designed for horizontal scalability. When you have a large volume of data (e.g., during an e-commerce sale), Kafka handles it by increasing the number of partitions (which are the actual disk spaces where data is written) and brokers.
•
Real-time Processing: Kafka is excellent for real-time data streaming and processing, commonly used for managing logs and analytics due to its offset management that ensures order and prevents duplication.
•
Integration with Other Tools:
◦
Kafka can capture logs and display them on logging tools like Splunk.
◦
Kafka Connect is a framework for integrating Kafka with external systems, providing connectors to ingest data to and from various sources and sinks (producers and consumers).
◦
Kafka Streams is a processing library that allows developers to build real-time applications and microservices using Kafka as the underlying infrastructure, enabling the processing and viewing of logs from backend applications.
•
Microservices Architecture: Kafka is frequently used as an asynchronous medium of communication between multiple microservices in backend applications.
•
