in real time how to create partitions, topics,.... either by using commands are java code.

✅ 1. Using Kafka CLI (Recommended for DevOps, Admins)
This is the most common method in production environments or during infrastructure setup.

▶️ Create a Topic with Partitions and Replication
# Syntax:
kafka-topics.sh --create --topic <topic-name> --bootstrap-server <host:port> --partitions <n> --replication-factor <n>
# Example:
kafka-topics.sh --create --topic orders --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --partitions 3 --replication-factor 1
-----------------------
✅ 2. Using Java Code (AdminClient API)
Kafka's AdminClient API allows you to create topics programmatically — useful for dynamic topic creation in microservices.

▶️ Maven Dependency
Make sure your pom.xml has:
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.apache.kafka</groupId>
    <artifactId>kafka-clients</artifactId>
    <version>3.7.0</version> <!-- or compatible -->
</dependency>

▶️ Java Code to Create Topic
import org.apache.kafka.clients.admin.AdminClient;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.admin.AdminClientConfig;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.admin.NewTopic;

import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Properties;

public class KafkaTopicCreator {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties config = new Properties();
        config.put(AdminClientConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "localhost:9092");

        try (AdminClient admin = AdminClient.create(config)) {
            NewTopic topic = new NewTopic("orders", 3, (short) 1); // 3 partitions, replication factor 1
            admin.createTopics(Collections.singleton(topic));
            System.out.println("Topic created successfully");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
✅ This method is good for on-demand topic creation from within services.

⚠️ What Happens If You Don't Create Topics?
Kafka has an option:
auto.create.topics.enable=true

If true (default in dev), topics will be automatically created when a producer/consumer tries to use them.
Not recommended for production — can lead to misconfiguration or accidental topic creation.

🔧 Code (Runtime Topic Creation)
public void createTopicIfNotExists(String topicName, int partitions, short replicationFactor) {
    Properties props = new Properties();
    props.put(AdminClientConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "localhost:9092");

    try (AdminClient adminClient = AdminClient.create(props)) {
        Set<String> existingTopics = adminClient.listTopics().names().get();

        if (!existingTopics.contains(topicName)) {
            NewTopic newTopic = new NewTopic(topicName, partitions, replicationFactor);
            adminClient.createTopics(Collections.singletonList(newTopic)).all().get();
            System.out.println("Created topic: " + topicName);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Topic already exists: " + topicName);
        }
    } catch (Exception e) {
        e.printStackTrace();
    }
}
✅ Best practice: Call this in your service startup or when new user/tenant is registered.

⚠️ Runtime Partition Increase (Only Increase Allowed)
Kafka does not allow decreasing partitions. But you can increase them using AdminClient.

🔧 Code (Increase Partitions)
public void increasePartitions(String topicName, int newPartitionCount) {
    Properties props = new Properties();
    props.put(AdminClientConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "localhost:9092");

    try (AdminClient adminClient = AdminClient.create(props)) {
        Map<String, NewPartitions> partitionMap = new HashMap<>();
        partitionMap.put(topicName, NewPartitions.increaseTo(newPartitionCount));

        adminClient.createPartitions(partitionMap).all().get();
        System.out.println("Partitions increased for topic: " + topicName);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        e.printStackTrace();
    }
}
⚠️ Only use this if you understand partition reassignment logic. Increasing partitions does not rebalance existing messages!

